  "bug": {
    "bug_id": "JacksonDatabind-21",
    "project": "JacksonDatabind",
    "summary": "No title",
    "description": "Currently, if I want to deserialize an enum with a value that isn't its \nEnum.name()\n, I can do either\n\npublic\n\nenum\n\nTestEnum\n{\n\nVALUE_ONE\n(\n\"value1\"\n);\n\nprivate\n\nString\n\nvalueInJson\n;\n\nprivate\n\nTestEnum\n(\nString\n\nvalueInJson\n) {\n\nthis\n.\nvalueInJson\n= \nvalueInJson\n;\n}\n\n@\nJsonCreator\n\npublic\n\nstatic\n\nTestEnum\n\ngetEnumFromValue\n(\nString\n\nvalue\n) {\n\nfor\n(\nTestEnum\n\ntestEnum\n: \nvalues\n()) {\n\nif\n(\ntestEnum\n.\nvalueInJson\n.\nequals\n(\nvalue\n)) {\n\nreturn\n\ntestEnum\n;\n}\n}\n\nthrow\n\nnew\n\nIllegalArgumentException\n();\n}\n}\n\nor, using \nDeserializationFeature.READ_ENUMS_USING_TO_STRING\n,\n\npublic\n\nenum\n\nTestEnum\n{\n\nVALUE_ONE\n(\n\"value1\"\n);\n\nprivate\n\nString\n\nvalueInJson\n;\n\nprivate\n\nTestEnum\n(\nString\n\nvalueInJson\n) {\n\nthis\n.\nvalueInJson\n= \nvalueInJson\n;\n}\n\n@\nOverride\n\npublic\n\nString\n\ntoString\n() {\n\nreturn\n\nvalueInJson\n;\n}\n}\n\nThis seems like a lot of boilerplate - is there a simpler way to do this, similar to how \nGson\nhandles it?\n\npublic\n\nenum\n\nTestEnum\n{\n\n@\nSerializedName\n(\n\"value1\"\n)\n\nVALUE_ONE\n\n}\n\nIt's both more concise and handles both serialization and deserialization."
  },
